# Generated artifacts — never text-merge (req-dev-validation-ratchet-harness).
#
# The guard baselines are GENERATED output (`manage.py guards --sync-<surface>`),
# not authored text. Git's default line-based merge happily UNIONS two sessions'
# regenerations when they touch different lines — no conflict, no review — and a
# unioned ratchet baseline is a SILENTLY WIDENED one: both sides' entries become
# permanently allowed without anyone deciding that. (The ratchet's stale-entry
# tooth catches this later in CI; `-merge` catches it here, at the merge, where
# the fix is obvious.)
#
# `-merge` tells git not to attempt a textual merge: when both sides changed the
# file it declares a conflict instead of inventing a union. Resolve by taking
# EITHER side and RE-RUNNING the generator — never by hand-merging output.
#
# Deliberately a built-in attribute, not a `merge=<driver>` custom driver: a
# named driver must be defined in each clone's local git config, so it would
# silently do nothing for contributors and in CI. `-merge` needs no config and
# therefore applies on every clone — the same reason enforcement lives in CI
# rather than in hooks (which need `core.hooksPath`, set only by spawn).
#
# Directory-wide glob on purpose: a new baseline added to this directory is
# covered automatically, so the protection cannot be forgotten.
tap/guards/baselines/*.txt -merge

# NOT covered, deliberately:
#   * the generated Validation Map block — it is embedded inside a prose spec
#     (`specs/spec-dev-validation.md`); attributes are per-file, so `-merge`
#     there would force conflicts on ordinary prose edits. Its exact-match
#     meta-test (`test_spec_map_in_sync`) already makes a hand-merged block
#     unlandable, and names the fix command in the failure message.
#   * `tap/guards/guard_manifest.txt` — an authored floor, not generated output.
#     A union merge of two sessions each adding a guard is the CORRECT result.
